course: dictionary
lesson: 5918
title: Marmots and what the clues tell you
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You can often work out a new word from the words around it. «Marmots are big ground squirrels of mountain meadows.» The words big, ground and squirrels are the clues: a marmot is a kind of squirrel that lives on the ground, and it is bigger than most squirrels.
explain: Look at what the animal does. «Marmots whistle loudly when they see an eagle or a fox, warning the others to run to their burrows.» Whistling to warn others and running to burrows tell you that a marmot is a watchful animal that lives in holes in the ground.
explain: Always check your guess. Put your meaning into the sentence in place of the new word. If it still makes sense, you are probably right. If it does not, read the sentence again, or look the word up.
fact: A marmot is a large, plump ground squirrel.
fact: Marmots live in mountains and grasslands in Europe, Asia and North America.
fact: Marmots dig burrows, and they hibernate through the winter.
fact: «Alpine marmots» live in the Alps and whistle to warn others of eagles or foxes.
fact: The groundhog of North America is a kind of marmot.
exercise: «Marmots are big ground squirrels of mountain meadows.» What is a marmot? | A fish that swims in rivers | A bird that flies over mountains | A type of tree | *A large, plump ground squirrel
exercise: «Marmots whistle loudly when they see an eagle or a fox.» Why do marmots whistle? | *To warn the others to run to their burrows | To scare away the rain | To call for food | To sing for fun at night
exercise: Marmots sleep through the whole winter. What is the word for this long winter sleep? | *Hibernating | Hunting | Migrating | Swimming
test: «Marmots almost vanished when most steppe was ploughed.» What does vanished mean? | Came out to play | Fell fast asleep | *Disappeared | Grew bigger
test: «Marmot families live in big burrows with many entrances.» Where do marmot families live? | In tall trees | On rooftops in towns | In the sea | *Under the ground, in burrows
exam: A child reads «sleep like a marmot» and guesses it means sleeping badly. What do the clues suggest? | Running all night | *Sleeping very deeply and for a long time | Eating a big dinner | Sleeping badly and waking up often
